// contrib/http/tls.mere -- serve HTTPS, terminating TLS in the process.
//
// SEPARATE FROM http.mere ON PURPOSE, and the reason is mechanical rather than
// stylistic. The C backend links OpenSSL into a program that DECLARES a TLS
// extern, and an `import` declares everything the imported module declares. So
// putting `http_serve_tls` in http.mere made every program that serves HTTP at
// all -- plaintext ones included -- require OpenSSL headers to build. That is
// the blanket dependency this project deliberately does not have: before
// v0.1.338 a plaintext contrib/http program needed no OpenSSL, and v0.1.338
// took that away without noticing, because the app that would have shown it
// (mere-blog) already links OpenSSL for Postgres.
//
// So: importing this module is the opt-in.
//
//   import "http/http.mere";
//   import "http/tls.mere";
//   let _ = http_serve_tls port cert_path key_path handler;
//
// `http_serve_tls port cert key handler` -- the certificate chain and key are
// PEM paths, loaded once before the socket is bound, so a certificate that
// cannot be used is an error at startup rather than a surprise on the first
// connection.
//
// After the handshake the handler is byte-identical to the plaintext one: the
// response path, `http_send_file`, and the per-request arena are all unchanged,
// because the runtime routes every byte of a served connection through one
// read/write pair.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. Until v0.1.338 a Mere program could dial a TLS connection but
// not answer one, so every web program written against contrib/http served
// cleartext. Nothing said so -- no README in the project recommended a proxy or
// mentioned TLS for serving at all. That is what made the gap durable: not
// advice that papered over it, but a question that never came up.
//
// C backend only. On the Node host, http.glue.js implements it with
// https.createServer, so the same program serves TLS on both.
extern fn http_serve_tls: int -> str -> str -> (str -> str) -> unit;
